WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.982 --> 00:00:07.437
[CHIME]

00:00:07.437 --> 00:00:09.770
SHELMITH KARIUKI: It has been
fourteen years since the phrase,

00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:12.740
"Data is the new
oil," was first used.

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:14.630
This phrase was
coined by Clive Humby,

00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:18.920
implying that, just as oil
needs to be refined into gas,

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:22.320
chemicals, plastics,
et cetera, data

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:26.280
must be broken down and
analyzed for it to have value.

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:28.040
But what if it is not?

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:32.390
Of what value is the data
if it is open, accessible,

00:00:32.390 --> 00:00:34.100
but not usable?

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:37.070
Hi, my name is Shelmith
Kariuki, and I'm here

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:41.450
to talk about a small project
I embarked on in March 2020.

00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:44.450
rKenyaCensus is a
package that contains

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:50.390
the 2019 Kenya population
and housing census results.

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:54.170
The population census
exercise was held in August,

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:55.940
and this was the
first time that Kenya

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:59.630
was using technology for
both cartographic mapping

00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:02.000
and enumeration.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.280
The survey questionnaire
about areas

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:07.610
such as population
characteristics, agriculture,

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:10.980
education, information,
communication, and technology,

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:15.290
henceforth referred
to as ICT, et cetera.

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:18.800
This data was published
in four PDF files which

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:21.350
can be found on
the Kenya National

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:24.230
Bureau of Statistics website.

00:01:24.230 --> 00:01:26.330
Now, is this data open?

00:01:26.330 --> 00:01:29.030
Yes, it is available
on the website.

00:01:29.030 --> 00:01:30.180
Is it accessible?

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:32.430
Yes, anyone can download it.

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:36.890
But is it usable in
its current format?

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:38.810
But why do we even care?

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:43.400
Why is usability important?

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:45.560
Let us look at one of
the aspects covered

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:47.600
in the survey, ICT.

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:49.100
We're going to look
at the influence

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:52.970
that ICT has had on education
in Kenya during the COVID

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:54.320
pandemic.

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:56.690
But first, a few statistics.

00:01:56.690 --> 00:01:58.890
According to a new
report by UNICEF,

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:00.920
two out of three
school-age children

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:03.540
do not have internet
access at home.

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:06.050
In fact, the number
of unconnected pupils

00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:09.620
is higher in Africa
and Asia as compared

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:11.720
to the rest of the world.

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:14.780
The first COVID case
was announced in Kenya

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:16.220
on 12th March.

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:18.590
And two days after,
the government

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:20.660
announced closure of
schools, and institutions

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:22.910
were encouraged to move
their learning online.

00:02:22.910 --> 00:02:25.765
The Ministry of Education
also made a directive

00:02:25.765 --> 00:02:27.140
for the curriculum
teaching to be

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:29.600
delivered via
radio, TV, YouTube,

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:31.610
and other digital platforms.

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:34.190
And so, online learning began.

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:36.470
Kenyans adapted very quickly.

00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:38.690
One University trained
their students and stuff

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:40.490
and also entered
into a partnership

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:42.830
with a mobile network
operator to ensure

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:45.680
that their staff received
cheaper data bundles

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:48.350
specifically for e-learning.

00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:50.900
Several other institutions
did their best to hitch

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:53.810
to their government directive.

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:57.830
Entertainment programs were
aired on TV during school hours

00:02:57.830 --> 00:02:59.510
and young kids
quickly learned how

00:02:59.510 --> 00:03:03.800
to use tools such as
Zoom and Google Hangout.

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:07.250
But then, online learning was
not going well for everyone.

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:09.770
Leaders from semiarid
areas came out

00:03:09.770 --> 00:03:12.320
claiming that online
learning in their areas

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:14.630
was just but a fallacy
because the area has

00:03:14.630 --> 00:03:17.450
lacked basic infrastructure.

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:20.360
So what basic
infrastructure is required

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:22.340
for smooth online learning?

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:25.410
First, one would need a
laptop or a mobile phone

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:28.520
to access materials in
an electronic format.

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:30.260
And these devices
have to actually

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:33.050
be connected to the internet
so that you can actually

00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:35.720
access this information
and electricity is

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:38.090
needed to power both.

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:40.010
A lot of students
took to social media

00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:43.400
to complain that online learning
was not working for them.

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:45.950
A lot claimed that they
lacked their minimum basic

00:03:45.950 --> 00:03:49.100
requirements needed to
partake in online learning.

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:52.190
Electricity has also
been a problem in Kenya,

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:55.110
even for us who have
access to electricity.

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:56.870
It's not reliable.

00:03:56.870 --> 00:03:59.570
I'm actually hoping that I'll
be done with this presentation

00:03:59.570 --> 00:04:00.740
by the time lights go off.

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:04.310
You can visit the
Kenya power Twitter

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:07.040
handle to just see
more of the complaints

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:10.490
that we channel
through that platform,

00:04:10.490 --> 00:04:15.480
complaining about blackouts
day in and day out.

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:18.120
Many families do not have
the devices and internet

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:22.110
access that enable children to
take part in remote schooling.

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:25.140
Let's take an example
of two extreme cases.

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:27.040
Manzi comes from
an average family.

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:29.400
She has a bedroom of
her own in the house.

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:32.400
Manzi's father pays for internet
at the end of each month

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:35.220
and he recently purchased
a new laptop for her.

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:37.230
Manzi attends
"Shule Kubwa", which is

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:41.430
a university based in the
capital city of Kenya, Nairobi.

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:44.790
Chali, on the other hand,
comes from a humble background.

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:47.130
His parents are not able
to pay school fees for him

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:50.327
and so he has to depend on
the area's bursary fund.

00:04:50.327 --> 00:04:52.410
And after emerging top in the Kenya

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:55.500
secondary national
examination, Chali

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:59.280
got a scholarship to
also attend "Shule Kubwa".

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:01.290
So Chali and Manzi,
together with some

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:05.010
of the friends they grew up
with, are in the same class.

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:07.290
And while in school,
they are subjected

00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:09.450
to the same conditions--
the same lecture

00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:13.750
room, the same lecture period,
the same exam, and so on.

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:15.750
But what happens now
that are at home?

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:17.880
Well, Manzi is
actually very happy.

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:22.380
She gets to seclude herself in
her room during class sessions,

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:24.540
and while on her break,
she's able to enjoy

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:27.160
her favorite comedy on Netflix.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:29.220
But then for Chali,
he's actually depressed.

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:33.600
He's even thinking of repeating
the current class because he

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:37.560
feels he has been left behind.

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:39.930
After six months,
schools reopened

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:42.450
for a small subset of
students-- those preparing

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:44.580
for the national examinations.

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:48.270
In Kenya, national
examinations take place

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:49.680
at the end of each year.

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:52.440
Now, an effective
education response

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:54.840
to the COVID-19
pandemic needs to be

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:57.150
built on evidence and data.

00:05:57.150 --> 00:05:59.880
For example, what do we
know about our population?

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:02.250
How many families own
radios and televisions?

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:04.890
And do children actually
use and actually

00:06:04.890 --> 00:06:07.900
learn from these
online platforms?

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:12.690
And that's why the development
of rKenyaCensus was important.

00:06:12.690 --> 00:06:15.430
Development of this package
involved three steps.

00:06:15.430 --> 00:06:18.570
The first one was downloading
the data from the International

00:06:18.570 --> 00:06:20.920
Bureau of Statistics website.

00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:25.380
The second one involved
cleaning, scraping, cleaning,

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:27.180
and manipulating the data.

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:31.110
I used the tabulator package
to scrape the data and tidyverse

00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:34.360
and tabulizer functions for
cleaning and manipulating it.

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:37.050
The third step was the
actual package development.

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:40.380
And I'm very grateful to the
authors of devtools, testthat,

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:43.770
and usethis, who have made
package development very easy.

00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:48.150
I'm also grateful to Hadley and Jenny
for the R packages book that

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:51.480
is written in such a
simple language, such

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:56.820
that it is easy to understand
some of the concepts covered.

00:06:56.820 --> 00:07:00.420
For consistency, the datasets
were named in a similar manner

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:02.390
as the tables in the
published documents.

00:07:02.390 --> 00:07:08.910
So V1_T2.1 is actually
Table 2.1 in Volume 1.

00:07:08.910 --> 00:07:10.950
Data cleaning and
manipulation skills

00:07:10.950 --> 00:07:12.820
are really important
in this task.

00:07:12.820 --> 00:07:16.080
I had to deal with a lot of
drilling and leading spaces,

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:18.270
extra white spaces,
blank spaces,

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:19.860
and other weird characters.

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:21.930
I also needed to
restructure the data

00:07:21.930 --> 00:07:25.110
by generating new variables
that distinguished counties

00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:27.300
from sub-counties.

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:30.523
I'm grateful to the R4DS
community, especially

00:07:30.523 --> 00:07:32.190
Scott and Stephen,
who are of great help

00:07:32.190 --> 00:07:35.340
when I encountered
errors while coding.

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:40.050
Well, I still battle
with projects up to date.

00:07:40.050 --> 00:07:42.640
I also created a small
shiny app where anyone,

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:45.330
especially those who do not
use R can download the data

00:07:45.330 --> 00:07:50.130
in either .csv or .xlxs format.

00:07:50.130 --> 00:07:52.260
Now, could some
of this data have

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:54.330
helped us to determine
whether Kenya

00:07:54.330 --> 00:07:56.340
was ready for online learning?

00:07:56.340 --> 00:07:58.540
Let's have a look.

00:07:58.540 --> 00:08:01.090
This map shows the
proportion of households

00:08:01.090 --> 00:08:03.850
where the main type of
lighting is electricity.

00:08:03.850 --> 00:08:07.600
The data can be found
in Volume 4, Table 2.19.

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:11.710
So in essence, in the data,
it's going to be V4_2.19.

00:08:11.710 --> 00:08:14.850
The darker the region,
the higher the proportion.

00:08:14.850 --> 00:08:17.380
The darkest region
is the capital city

00:08:17.380 --> 00:08:19.090
of Kenya, which is Nairobi.

00:08:19.090 --> 00:08:20.950
Most regions in
the semiarid areas,

00:08:20.950 --> 00:08:23.230
that's the light
yellow regions, use

00:08:23.230 --> 00:08:26.290
either burned wood,
torches, or paraffin lamps

00:08:26.290 --> 00:08:27.730
as a source of light.

00:08:27.730 --> 00:08:29.920
Chali comes from
one of these areas.

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:32.350
In case anyone is
curious, these maps

00:08:32.350 --> 00:08:35.710
are created using
sf and ggplot2.

00:08:35.710 --> 00:08:37.539
The pattern is the
same when we look

00:08:37.539 --> 00:08:41.080
at access to
computers or laptops

00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:42.700
and usage of the internet.

00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:45.580
Semiarid and regions
that have high-population

00:08:45.580 --> 00:08:48.700
based in the rural areas
tend to be at a disadvantage.

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:51.460
There's no way Chali could have
reaped the benefits of online

00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:53.800
learning as much as
Manzi wouldn't have,

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:55.810
even at a bare minimum.

00:08:55.810 --> 00:08:59.380
If this kind of analysis
was available beforehand,

00:08:59.380 --> 00:09:01.330
the insights obtained
could have helped

00:09:01.330 --> 00:09:05.350
the government and stakeholders
make better decisions regarding

00:09:05.350 --> 00:09:08.710
learning during
the COVID pandemic.

00:09:08.710 --> 00:09:11.710
The data has been used by
the International Center

00:09:11.710 --> 00:09:14.730
for Humanitarian Affairs,
which is part of the Red Cross,

00:09:14.730 --> 00:09:18.340
to assess COVID-19
high-risk areas in Kenya.

00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:20.380
It's my prayer
that most entities

00:09:20.380 --> 00:09:24.130
will adopt this data to
develop insights that will help

00:09:24.130 --> 00:09:26.650
drive development in Kenya.

00:09:26.650 --> 00:09:30.250
And to conclude, we
have only touched

00:09:30.250 --> 00:09:33.040
on the ICT part of this data.

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:36.160
In essence, only 2
out of 74 tables.

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:37.900
Imagine how much
information we could

00:09:37.900 --> 00:09:41.050
get if we analyzed
all the census data,

00:09:41.050 --> 00:09:43.300
and imagine how much more
information and insights

00:09:43.300 --> 00:09:46.030
we could get if we
converted all the data sets

00:09:46.030 --> 00:09:49.900
that align in various websites
into machine-readable formats

00:09:49.900 --> 00:09:52.390
that are easy to analyze.

00:09:52.390 --> 00:09:54.490
Insights obtained
from this data will

00:09:54.490 --> 00:09:58.900
help the country prepare itself
better for the next calamity.

00:09:58.900 --> 00:10:02.350
R is an open source language and there
are so many learning resources

00:10:02.350 --> 00:10:03.130
online.

00:10:03.130 --> 00:10:05.650
It's up to us as our
developers and our users

00:10:05.650 --> 00:10:07.210
to use the knowledge
and skills we

00:10:07.210 --> 00:10:11.530
have to develop tools that will
help us in solving problems

00:10:11.530 --> 00:10:14.300
that exist in our society.

00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:15.910
Thank you for listening to me.

00:10:15.910 --> 00:10:16.880
Questions are welcome.

00:10:23.180 --> 00:10:24.730
[CHIME]

